    src=cmd/echo.c,third_party/sbase/echo.c

# SYNOPSIS

```usage
echo [arg ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`echo` writes its arguments separated by blanks and terminated by a newline on standard output.
With no <arg> it writes the newline alone.

`echo` takes no flags: `-n` is printed like any other <arg>, and the newline is always written.

In the POSIX namespace `echo` is sbase's command instead (see {The POSIX command|#posix}).

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% echo /tmp/*.x; echo '/tmp/*.x'
/tmp/a.x /tmp/b.x
/tmp/*.x
```

# SEE ALSO

rc(1)

# DIAGNOSTICS

The exit string is empty.

# BUGS

A failed write is not reported: the exit string is empty whatever happens to the output.

@node=posix title="The POSIX command"

```text
echo [-n] [string ...]
```

In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)), `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so `echo` runs sbase's command, as POSIX specifies.
It writes each <string> separated by blanks and terminated by a newline on standard output, and exits non-zero if the write fails.
It does not interpret backslashes.

: `-n`
  Do not write the newline; only a first argument of exactly `-n` is taken as the flag.

sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/echo.1` in the repository.
